      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to find the decimal sequence of the fraction 1/7. We can do this by dividing 1 by 7 and observing the result.\n\n1 ÷ 7 = 0.142857142857...\n\nNotice that this is a repeating decimal, with the same pattern repeating endlessly. The repeating sequence is \"142857\". The length of this sequence is 6.\n\nSince we are asked to find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine how many complete cycles of the repeating sequence fit into the 100 digits and what the remainder is. We can use the modulo operation to find the remainder when we divide 100 by 6.\n\n100 ÷ 6 = 16 remainder 4\n\nSo, the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \"142857\". Looking at the sequence, we can see that the 4th digit is \"2\".\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of 1/7 is $\\boxed{2}$.",
